  "title": "Fine-mapping autoimmune disease Caliskan M et al., 2021",
  "bodyHtml": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span>This table shows the effector genes associated with autoimmune disease, from the review:</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>A catalog of GWAS fine-mapping efforts in autoimmune disease.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>Caliskan M,&nbsp;</span><em>et al.</em></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>American Journal of Human Genetics. 2021 Apr; 108(4):549-563. doi:&nbsp;</span><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajhg.2021.03.009\"><span>https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ajhg.2021.03.009</span></a><span>&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>PMID: 33798443</span></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p dir=\"ltr\"><span>In this paper, the authors employed a combination of text mining and systematic review techniques to create a catalog of 85 studies focused on fine mapping of autoimmune Genome-Wide Association Study (GWAS) loci. They identified 230 GWAS loci encompassing allelic heterogeneity estimates and predictions of causal variants and genes relevant to various autoimmune diseases. These loci were associated with 15 different autoimmune diseases, yielding 455 combinations of locus-disease association signals. Using these estimates, the authors evaluated the likelihood of mediating disease risk associations across genes within GWAS loci, leading to the identification of robust signals related to the causal biology of autoimmune diseases. This comprehensive catalog is expected to be a valuable resource for consolidating and distilling the wealth of information in the autoimmune disease research field, ultimately aiding in the development of therapeutic strategies.</span></p>",
